// XFAIL: RT-471: `r?` on `let r: Result<i32, _> = Err(e);` with `let e = 4000000000;` later read as u32, in a function returning `Result<i32, i64>`, is refused ("integer literal 4000000000 does not fit in type 'i32'", "expected 'u32', got 'i32'"); rustc types e u32 (From<u32> for i64) and prints `err 4000000000` (0.30.2)
// From the re-review of the integer-literal work (t302int). Since v0.30.1 at least.
// On the release build `let o = Some(e); let c: u32 = e;` is refused the
// same way; the t302int branch accepts that and `Err(e)` without `?`,
// and still refuses this one: the `?` conversion types e i32.
// EXPECT: err 4000000000
fn f() -> Result<i32, i64> {
    let e = 4000000000;
    let r: Result<i32, _> = Err(e);
    let v = r?;
    let c: u32 = e;
    Ok(v + (c - c) as i32)
}

fn main() {
    match f() {
        Ok(v) => println("ok {}", v),
        Err(e) => println("err {}", e),
    }
}
